@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy";
}

<h1>Privacy</h1>

<p class="pitch">Your job search is your business. Here's what JobHunt HQ stores, why, and how you get it back or get rid of it.</p>

<section class="dashboard-section">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
        JobHunt HQ is an independent personal project, built and maintained by
        one developer. It stores the job-search information you put into it —
        nothing more — so it can run your pipeline, check postings against
        your criteria, and build your AI packets. Your data is never sold,
        never shared for advertising, and never used to train models.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="dashboard-section">
    <h2>What the app stores</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Your account:</strong> your email address and your
            password, stored as a hash by ASP.NET Core Identity — the app
            never sees or keeps your plain-text password. A sign-in cookie
            keeps you logged in on your device.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your applications:</strong> the companies, roles, statuses,
            dates, notes, and pay figures you enter, plus the posting text you
            paste in or fetch from a link, and the fit verdicts the app
            calculates for you.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your search profile:</strong> your target roles, pay floor,
            must-haves, deal-breakers, skip list, and standing answers —
            written by you, used only to check your postings and fill your
            packets.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your resumes:</strong> the version names and notes you add,
            and the PDF files you upload. Uploaded PDFs live in private Azure
            Blob Storage and are only ever served back through the app, to
            you, while you're signed in — there are no public links to them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Basic technical telemetry:</strong> the app records
            requests, page loads, and errors (Azure Application Insights) so
            the developer can tell when something is broken. That's for
            keeping the lights on, not for tracking you around the web — there
            are no advertising trackers.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="dashboard-section">
    <h2>What the app does with it</h2>
    <p>
        Your data powers the features you use: the pipeline board and
        dashboard, fit checks against your own criteria, the preparation
        views, and a daily email digest of your own follow-ups that are due.
        When you use "Fetch from URL" on a posting, the app downloads that
        page to extract its text — it fetches the link you gave it, nothing
        else.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>AI packets are made only when you ask.</strong> A packet is
        generated at the moment you click download, by the app itself. The
        app does not send your information to ChatGPT, Claude, or any other
        AI service — you decide whether to hand a packet to your own AI, and
        where. And as everywhere else in JobHunt HQ: the app never submits an
        application for you and never logs into a job board or an employer's
        site as you.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="dashboard-section">
    <h2>Exporting your data</h2>
    <p>
        Signed in, go to your <strong>Search profile</strong> page and choose
        <strong>Download my data</strong>. You get one JSON file with your
        search profile, every application with all its fields, and your
        resume version details. Resume PDF files aren't part of that export —
        you can download each of them from the Resumes page.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="dashboard-section">
    <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
        You can delete any individual application or resume in the app at any
        time, and deleting a resume also removes its uploaded PDF.
    </p>
    <p>
        To close your account entirely, use <strong>Account → Personal data →
        Delete</strong> and follow the steps there; that permanently deletes
        your sign-in account. Because your applications, resumes, and search
        profile live in the app's own tables — separate from the sign-in
        account — the clean order is: download your data if you want a copy,
        delete your applications and resumes in the app, then delete the
        account. Anything left behind is tied to an account that no longer
        exists and can't be signed into or accessed by anyone.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="dashboard-section">
    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
        If this page changes, the updated version will be posted here. The
        principles won't quietly change: your data stays yours, and the app
        stays preparation-only.
    </p>
</section>
